/// What somebody drew ON a site capture, kept BESIDE it rather than burned
/// into it.
///
/// THE DECISION THIS WHOLE FILE RESTS ON: the pixels are never modified.
/// The obvious build is to flatten the drawing into a new JPEG and store
/// that, and it is wrong here for a reason this schema already has a rule
/// about. A site photo is an evidence record — identity fields locked after
/// creation, sent correspondence closes but never deletes — and flattening
/// either destroys the original or silently forks it into two files that
/// disagree. Keeping the marks as rows means the photograph stays exactly
/// what the camera produced, the markup stays editable and dated, and both
/// can be shown together.
///
/// WHAT THAT COSTS, stated here because it is real and not yet paid: the
/// raw blob URL still serves an UNMARKED photo. Anyone who downloads the
/// file, rather than reading the page, gets the picture without the arrows.
/// The app therefore never offers a marked-up photo's file as "the photo" —
/// it says "original, without markup" — and the fix, a flattened export, is
/// the same piece of work as the PDF report that has to exist before a
/// marked-up photo is ever ATTACHED to anything. It is deliberately not
/// faked in the meantime.
enum JobMediaAnnotationKind {
  /// A line with a head, pointing at the thing being talked about.
  ARROW

  /// A rectangle around a region. "Highlight" in the product, `BOX` here,
  /// because it is a shape and not a colour.
  BOX

  /// A word or a sentence placed at a point — "их conduit", "rework".
  TEXT

  /// A line whose LABEL is a distance the person typed.
  ///
  /// THE APP DOES NOT MEASURE ANYTHING, and this comment is the only place
  /// that can stop somebody assuming it does. There is no scale reference
  /// in a jobsite photograph and no perspective correction here, so a
  /// pixel length is not a distance and cannot be converted into one. What
  /// this stores is a LINE the person drew and a STRING the person typed —
  /// their claim about a distance, on their own authority, exactly like the
  /// number they would have written on a printout in marker. Computing a
  /// figure and showing it to a GC as though the software had measured it
  /// would be inventing evidence.
  MEASURE
}

/// One mark on one capture.
///
/// A ROW PER SHAPE rather than one JSON blob per photo, which is the other
/// obvious build. Three reasons, in order of how much they matter:
///
///   - each mark carries its OWN author and timestamp. On a photo two
///     people annotated across a fortnight, "who said this arrow points at
///     their damage, and when" is answerable. Inside a blob it is not;
///   - the marks are then ordinary rows that a query can count, cap and
///     order, rather than a document the application has to parse before it
///     knows how many there are;
///   - a blob is a second place for shape-validity to be enforced, and it
///     is enforced nowhere: a malformed JSON payload becomes a row that
///     every later reader has to defend against. `lib/job-media-annotations.ts`
///     validates a shape once, on the way in, and the columns below are
///     what survived.
model JobMediaAnnotation {
  id String @id @default(cuid())

  /// CASCADE, unlike most of this schema's RESTRICT children, and the
  /// distinction is the one `JobMediaTagAssignment` already draws: a mark
  /// is not a record that would be silently lost with its parent, it is
  /// part of the parent. Deleting a photo must not be blocked because
  /// somebody drew on it, and a mark pointing at a photo that no longer
  /// exists is not evidence of anything.
  ///
  /// It also keeps this model out of the cleanup scripts entirely — the
  /// trap #224 and #228 both fell into is a per-job RESTRICT child that
  /// blocks the job delete, and a CASCADE child of `JobMedia` is reached by
  /// the `jobMedia.deleteMany` both scripts already run.
  mediaId String
  media   JobMedia @relation(fields: [mediaId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  kind JobMediaAnnotationKind

  /// WHERE, IN FRACTIONS OF THE IMAGE, never in pixels.
  ///
  /// 0..1 on each axis, relative to the image's own intrinsic size. A photo
  /// is rendered at whatever width the card, the gallery, the portal or a
  /// phone in landscape happens to give it, and a pixel coordinate is only
  /// meaningful next to the size it was captured at — which would mean
  /// storing that size too and trusting it forever. A fraction survives
  /// every one of those without a second column to disagree with.
  ///
  /// `x2`/`y2` are the far end of a line or the opposite corner of a box,
  /// and are NULL for `TEXT`, which is a point. Nullable rather than
  /// duplicating `x1`/`y1` into them: a text mark has no second point, and
  /// writing one in would be a value that reads as real and means nothing.
  x1 Float
  y1 Float
  x2 Float?
  y2 Float?

  /// The words. Required in practice for `TEXT` and `MEASURE` and refused
  /// for neither at the database level, because the rule is per kind and
  /// lives in the one pure module both writers import.
  label String?

  /// Who drew it. SET NULL on user delete, the same choice `capturedBy` and
  /// `sharedWithClientBy` make: the mark outlives the account, and losing
  /// the name is better than losing the mark.
  createdByUserId String?
  createdBy       User?   @relation("JobMediaAnnotationBy", fields: [createdByUserId], references: [id])

  createdAt DateTime @default(now())

  /// Every read is "the marks on this photo", in a stable order.
  @@index([mediaId, createdAt])
}
